drm: h713: afbd: the capture slot from the capture's own index, not the firmware's flip pointers

The video plane in passthrough and the flip notifier (the capture driver,
and through it the warp) took the slot to show from the firmware's flip
pointers at AFBD +0x320/+0x324, sampled on every vsync. With a source at the
panel's size that works: the pointers name the slot being filled, a few
hundred lines behind the capture, and every frame reaches the panel. With a
1080p60 source scaled down to a 720p panel they do not: the pointer names a
slot finished one to three frames earlier, holds it for two vsyncs every
fourth or fifth frame and skips the next - 43 to 57 of 60 frames shown, and
the loss grows over hours (doku/197, 198). Measured against the slots'
contents with a moving picture, the capture itself writes every frame, in
strict rotation A, B, C, and a finished slot stays untouched for two more
frame periods; only the publisher fails (doku/200).

The capture says where it is: INCAP +0xa0c is the index of the slot it is
writing, it steps at every source frame boundary in the order of the slot
table at +0x8f0 (luma) / +0x930 (chroma, addresses >> 4). So the driver
chooses the slot from that index, through the read-only INCAP syscon the
capture driver already reads its geometry from (allwinner,incap, new on the
display node; by compatible for a tree from before the property):

  - the notifier gets the slot finished last (index - 1), whole now and
    for two more frame periods; its consumer reads within milliseconds;
  - the plane gets the slot being written now: addresses queued during a
    frame are fetched from the next vsync on, by when it is finished, and
    the capture returns to it two frame periods later with the fetch at
    the bottom. The finished slot would tear here - in every beat of the
    two clocks there is a stretch of vsyncs where its fetch would start
    just as the capture starts overwriting it, and the capture is faster;
  - a slow source (30 Hz into the 60 Hz panel) turns that around: the slot
    being written is not finished by the next vsync, the finished one stays
    whole for three of its periods. The ratio of source frames to vsyncs
    over 32 vsyncs decides, threshold 0.8.

ring_select (module parameter, 0644) keeps the pointers reachable: 0 is the
old behaviour, 1 the index, the default where the syscon is there.

Measured 11.10.2026 on the HY300PRO-G-EU (720p panel, module swapped,
arbeit/aufnahme-takt-20261011 anzeige.py: the plane's address register,
the INCAP frame counter and the slot order over /dev/mem): a moving 1080p60
source, before 32.9 frames a second shown with 25.2 skips, after 59.70 shown
with 0.30 skips over 60 s - the one frame in 200 that a 59.71 Hz panel
cannot show of a 60.00 Hz source; the warp 59.7 fps with none dropped
(before 57.9 on a fresh boot, 30-41 after hours). 1080p30 shows 30.00,
1080p50 50.00 (the pointers: 26), 720p60 59.75, each frame once. Picture
clean under motion (owner's eyes). The pointers still move only 41-47 times
a second beside it.

